      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nTo understand water fully, it helps to look at it from a few different perspectives: chemical, physical, and ecological.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Perspective (What is it made of?)\nScientifically, water is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n\nThis means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are held together by strong covalent bonds. Furthermore, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. Because of this, water molecules act like microscopic magnets, clinging to each other (which creates surface tension) and allowing many other substances to dissolve easily within it (making it the \"universal solvent\").\n\n### 2. The Physical Perspective (How does it behave?)\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature (found in oceans, rivers, and rain).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n    * *Fun Fact:* Unlike almost every other substance, water **expands** and becomes less dense when it freezes, which is why ice floats on water.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\nPure water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless.\n\n### 3. The Ecological Perspective (Why does it matter?)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water), as well as in lakes, rivers, glaciers, and underground aquifers. \n\nWater is the foundation of life:\n*   **Biology:** The human body is made of about 60% water. Cells require water to transport nutrients, chemical reactions, and waste products. Without water, biological life as we know it cannot survive.\n*   **Climate:** Water regulates the Earth's temperature. Oceans absorb heat from the sun and distribute"
